description.narrative:
Three year pan-African project to mobilise capital for climate resilience and job creation by strengthening financial markets. 
It addresses two urgent challenges: Africa’s climate finance gap (only ~20% of adaptation needs met) and the need to create 50,000 jobs daily. 

The overall objective is to catalyse systemic change, so African financial systems mobilise domestic and international capital for adaptation, nature-positive investment, and private-sector-led growth.

Project activities: 
(i) an Early-Stage Venture Finance Platform to pilot adaptation finance models; 
(ii) a Nature Finance InnovationLab to advance ecosystem-based projects to bankability; 
(iii) an SME Listed Fund in Rwanda to provide local-currency growth capital; 
(iv) support for 10+ sustainable bond issuances in Kenya, Nigeria, Tanzania, Senegal, Côte d’Ivoire, Botswana and Morocco; 
(v) capacity building for fund managers (PAFMA) and pension regulators (APSA); 
(vi) Africa-wide and country-level Climate Finance Landscape studies.

Expected project results: US$300m catalysed (85% local currency, 50% institutional capital); 17 innovative finance structures operationalised; 450+ SMEs financed; 400+ trustees, regulators and financial institution staff trained on sustainable investments;
and 8+ toolkits or guidance notes disseminated to support replication, leading to 5,000+ jobs created, access to basic services for 19.3m people and improved resilience for 15.4m people.

The project is ODA-eligible, targeting private-sector development and climate adaptation in low- and middle-income African countries. 
Partnerships include stock exchanges and regulators, industry associations, Moody’s Analytics, Climate Bonds Initiative,and Climate Policy Initiative, ensuring reach, sustainability, and policy uptake.
